# brief-web

@lede
The static-site generator for Brief. To Brief what `mdBook` is to
Markdown — a separate executable, a separate crate, the same compiler.
@end

## What it is

`brief-web` reads a directory of `.brf` files plus a `book.toml` and
produces a navigable HTML site. Single-page mode renders one document.
Multi-page mode renders a `SUMMARY.brf`-driven sidebar of pages.

This site you are reading is built with `brief-web`.

## The three commands

```bash
brief-web init [PATH]    # scaffold a new project (--multi for multi-page)
brief-web build [PATH]   # one-shot build into dist/
brief-web serve [PATH]   # build + live-reload dev server
```

`PATH` defaults to the current directory.

## A quickstart

```bash
brief-web init my-site --multi
cd my-site
brief-web serve
# open http://127.0.0.1:3000
```

The dev server watches `src/`, `book.toml`, and `theme/` (debounced to
about 150 ms), rebuilds on change, and pushes a reload over a Server-
Sent-Events stream. The default theme injects a tiny client that listens
for the event.

## Project layout

```text
my-site/
├── book.toml          (site config)
├── src/
│   ├── SUMMARY.brf    (optional: presence = multi-page mode)
│   ├── index.brf
│   └── chapters/
│       └── intro.brf
├── theme/             (optional: overrides built-in theme)
│   ├── page.html
│   └── style.css
└── dist/              (gitignored, build output)
```

Without `SUMMARY.brf`, `brief-web` runs in single-page mode and renders
only `src/index.brf`.

## `book.toml`

```toml
[book]
title       = "My docs"
description = "What this site is about."
authors     = ["You"]

[build]
src = "src"
out = "dist"

[site]
base_url = "/"

[server]
host = "127.0.0.1"
port = 3000

# Site-scoped shortcodes (extend, do not replace, brief.toml).
# [shortcodes.tweet]
# kind = "inline"
# template_html = "..."
```

## `SUMMARY.brf`

The file *is* the navigation tree. List items become pages; nested lists
become nested navigation:

```brief
# Summary

## Get started

- @ref[introduction.brf](Introduction)
- @ref[install.brf](Installation)

## Reference

- @ref[grammar.brf](Grammar)
- @ref[shortcodes.brf](Shortcodes)
```

Headings at level 2 or higher become section dividers in the sidebar.
The `# Summary` H1 is ignored.

## Cross-page links: `@ref`

`brief-web` uses Brief's built-in `@ref` shortcode for cross-page links:

```brief
See @ref[getting-started/install.brf](Installation) for the next step.
See @ref[reference/grammar.brf#headings](Headings) for the heading rules.
```

The path is project-relative (rooted at `brief.toml` if present, or the
build root otherwise). An `#anchor` suffix targets a heading anchor in
the destination page; missing files or anchors are build errors. See
@ref[reference/built-in-shortcodes.brf](Built-in shortcodes).

## Themes

`brief-web` ships an embedded default theme (light/dark via
`prefers-color-scheme`, sticky sidebar, mobile collapse).

To override, drop `theme/page.html` and/or `theme/style.css` into your
project. The template is mustache-style with these placeholders:

@t
| Placeholder | Meaning
| `{{ title }}` | Page title — H1, falling back to SUMMARY title
| `{{ description }}` | First paragraph, used for `<meta description>`
| `{{ site_title }}` | `[book].title` from `book.toml`
| `{{ content }}` | Rendered HTML for the page body
| `{{ sidebar }}` | Rendered HTML for the navigation sidebar
| `{{ base_url }}` | `[site].base_url`
| `{{ stylesheet }}` | URL of `style.css`
| `{{ reload_script }}` | Live-reload `<script>`; empty during `build`

No conditionals or loops. Logic that wants them belongs in a Brief
shortcode, not in the template.

## Live reload

`brief-web serve` runs an initial build, starts an HTTP server, and
watches the project for changes. On change:

1. Rebuild the affected files (in v1 — the whole site).
2. Push a reload event over the SSE stream at `/__brief_web_events`.
3. The injected client refreshes the page.

No WebSockets, no polling. Disable with `--no-watch` if you want a
serve-only run.

## What's not in here

The following are out of scope:

- Client-side search.
- WASM playground.
- Print view.
- Multi-language / i18n.
- RSS / Atom feeds.
- Plugin / preprocessor protocol.

Extension goes through Brief's standard shortcode mechanism, registered
in `book.toml`. That is the extension surface.

## Why a separate binary

The compiler is a library. `brief-cli` and `brief-web` both depend on
it. The split exists so a docs author who only wants to render a site
doesn't have to think about the compiler's CLI; conversely, an LLM
pipeline using `brief compile --target=llm` doesn't need a static-site
generator's worth of code on disk.
